---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { release } from '../data/releases';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy & local-first | Slate"
  description={`How Slate ${release.version} stores clips locally, handles user-initiated captures, and keeps the core experience independent of accounts and cloud services.`}
  home={false}
>
  <article class="shell privacy-page">
    <p class="eyebrow">SLATE {release.version} / PRIVACY</p>
    <h1>
      Your memory.
      <br />
      <span>On your computer.</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="privacy-intro">
      Slate's core experience is local-first. Here's what that means and what it
      doesn't.
    </p>
    <section>
      <h2>Local storage, not a cloud library</h2>
      <p>
        Clips, groups, and source metadata are stored in a local plaintext
        SQLite database. Slate does not require an account or send those records
        to a Slate server.
      </p>
      <p>
        The database is not encrypted by Slate. Windows account security, device
        encryption, filesystem access, and backups remain your responsibility.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>You choose what to capture</h2>
      <p>
        Browser selection capture happens when you invoke Save or Save
        selection. Save this page stores the current HTTP/HTTPS URL and page
        title as a Link clip, not a copy of the entire page. Saved URLs can
        include sensitive query parameters or fragments.
      </p>
      <p>
        Browser capture uses Native Messaging to reach the local installed host.
        Desktop selected-text capture uses a user-invoked Copy action and
        attempts to restore the previous clipboard state; some applications or
        clipboard formats can prevent capture or restoration.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>No app analytics or telemetry</h2>
      <p>
        Slate {release.version} contains no analytics or telemetry SDK and does
        not upload clip content, usage events, or diagnostics. Websites you
        visit and source links you choose to open still make their normal
        network requests independently.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Copy, sources, and data retention</h2>
      <p>
        Copy puts chosen clip content on the Windows clipboard. Open source
        opens the stored URL in your browser. Ordinary uninstall retains the
        library; choosing Delete app data in the uninstaller removes it. Keep a
        backup of information you need.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>This website</h2>
      <p>
        This site has no account forms, analytics, tracking scripts, or cookies
        added by the application. A hosting provider receives ordinary web
        requests to serve the site; that is separate from Slate's local clip
        storage.
      </p>
    </section>
    <a class="text-link" href="/">
      Back to Slate <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
    </a>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
